<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yalta Conference</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Yaltinskaya konferentsiya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yalta Conference, held 4–11 February 1945 near Yalta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> meeting of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>, Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> and General Secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> on postwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The three agreed that Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> would be split into occupation zones, with a fourth for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s eastern border would follow the Curzon Line, with compensation in the west from Germany. Stalin pledged that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> would enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> three months after the defeat of Germany and join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> dividing Europe, the conference became a subject of intense controversy.</p></div>
